<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Scharnhorst</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerhard von Scharnhorst was a Hanoverian-born Prussian general and military reformer, trained at Count William of Schaumburg-Lippe's military school on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmstein">Wilhelmstein</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1806 he led the reform of its army with Gneisenau, creating a general staff, opening officer careers to merit and preparing a reserve. He was wounded at Lützen in 1813 and died soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst.jpg" alt="Gerhard von Scharnhorst" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circle of Friedrich Bury · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Bury_-_Bildnis_des_Generals_von_Scharnhorst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerhard Johann David von Scharnhorst was born on 12 November 1755 at Bordenau near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, into a minor landowning family. He educated himself and gained admission to the military academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-count-of-schaumburg-lippe">William, Count of Schaumburg-Lippe</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmstein">Wilhelmstein</a> fortress. He received a Hanoverian commission in 1778, moved to the artillery in 1783 and taught at the new artillery school in Hanover.</p><p>He founded a military journal that lasted until 1805 and wrote handbooks for officers, since writing was his chief income. His first campaign was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1793, where he served under the Duke of York at the defence of Menen. In 1801 he accepted a Prussian offer at twice his Hanoverian pay, on condition that he keep his seniority and receive a pension and a patent of nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> he taught at the military academy, where Clausewitz was among his pupils, and founded the Berlin Military Society. In 1806 he was chief of staff to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, was lightly wounded at Auerstedt on 14 October, and went into captivity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> at Ratekau on 7 November before being quickly exchanged. He helped lead L'Estocq's corps beside the Russians and received the order Pour le Mérite for Eylau in February 1807.</p><p>He became a major-general soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Peace of Tilsit</a> and headed a reform commission with Gneisenau, Grolman and Boyen, joined by Stein. The commission converted the professional long-service army wrecked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> into a national one. Foreign enlistment was abolished, corporal punishment was limited to flagrant insubordination, promotion for merit was established, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landwehr">Landwehr</a> reserves were begun. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> grew suspicious and Frederick William III repeatedly suspended the reforms, and universal service had not been secured by Scharnhorst's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1811 and 1812, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> into an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Scharnhorst left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on unlimited leave and wrote a work on firearms. After the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he returned to the king's headquarters as chief of staff to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>. He was wounded in the foot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> on 2 May 1813, and the wound, worsened by the retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, proved fatal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 28 June 1813, where he had gone to negotiate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s intervention.</p><p>He had just been promoted lieutenant-general. Frederick William III had a statue of him by Christian Daniel Rauch put up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and he was buried at the Invalidenfriedhof. The German armoured cruiser of 1906, the battleship of 1936 and the East German Scharnhorst Order bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerhard von Scharnhorst” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gerhard von Scharnhorst</b> and August von Gneisenau rebuilt it with a general staff, promotion by merit and the Landwehr militia, and Prussian troops under Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher fought at Leipzig in 1813 and Waterloo in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Count William, in the 18th century, was a military reformer who built the island fortress of Wilhelmstein in the Steinhuder Meer and ran a school for officers where <b>Gerhard von Scharnhorst</b> trained.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bordenau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.46,9.48&amp;year=1792" aria-label="Bordenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4763 354 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gerhard von Scharnhorst"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5213 624h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5213 624h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.46,9.48&amp;year=1792">What was here in 1792? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
